Article 7

7 - (1) The Regional Government carries out the prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter a through empowerment and awareness-raising for Families, Parents, and the Community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

2. In addition to the empowerment and awareness-raising as referred to in paragraph (1), it is carried out by means of:
a. providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in education, both formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services in accordance with applicable provisions;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern for the prevention of and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement officials, government officials, educational institutions, and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
g. opening an integrated service system for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in every kelurahan (urban village).

Article 3

The purpose of protecting women and children from acts of violence, is to:

a. prevent acts of violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of acts of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect, provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children from acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; dane. strengthen women and children victims of acts of violence to be more empowered physically, psychologically, socially, and economically.

6 -

Article 10

The Regional Government carries out recovery as referred to in Article 6 letter c, including:

a. providing physical recovery at health service institutions;
b. providing medicolegal services;
c. assisting the repatriation of victims;
d. providing temporary protection in Safe Houses (safe house);
e. providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;
f. providing spiritual guidance services; and/or
g. preparing family, school, work and community environments, as well as economic empowerment.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILD VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 18

Women and child victims of acts of violence receive the following rights:

a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and according to needs;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance; and
j. the right to security and protection.

Article 8

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 7 is carried out by Regional Apparatus administering governmental affairs in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. population administration and civil registration;
f. women's empowerment, protection of women and children;
g. cooperatives and Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises; and
h. public tranquility, public order, and community protection

(2) Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on the RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 16

Every person who hears, sees, or knows of the occurrence of violence against women and children is obligated to:

a. prevent and stop the continuation of acts of violence;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and/or
d. assist in the process of submitting an application for a protection order.

Article 14

1. Obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a include:
a. establishing and implementing policies and programs, and conducting cooperation activities in the administration of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutions for the protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
c. allocating a budget for the administration of the protection of women and children from acts of violence according to regional financial capacity;
d. fostering and supervising the administration of the protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and child victims of acts of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and
g. appointing parents and/or substitute family caregivers as a protective measure for children who are victims of acts of violence.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent establishes programs and activities for the protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
3. The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) constitutes part of the RPJMD.
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of protection of women and children from acts of violence includes:

a. organization of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. obligations and responsibilities;
c. forms of violence;
d. rights of women and children from acts of violence;
e. institutions;
f. cooperation and partnerships;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. awards; andi. funding;

Article 23

1. The Regional Government develops cooperation patterns for the protection of women and children from violence.
